<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Cochrane, 10th Earl of Dundonald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lord Cochrane, Thomas Cochrane</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Cochrane, Lord Cochrane and later 10th Earl of Dundonald, was a British naval officer who commanded the navies of independence movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. In 1820 he carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a>'s Liberating Expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and as admiral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>'s navy he helped suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-equator">Confederation of the Equator</a> in 1824. He later served the Greek war of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald.jpg" alt="Thomas Cochrane, 10th Earl of Dundonald" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Ramsay (1789–1854) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lord_Cochrane,_by_James_Ramsay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Cochrane was born at Annsfield, near Hamilton in South Lanarkshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, on 14 December 1775, the son of Archibald, Lord Cochrane, who became the 9th Earl of Dundonald in 1778. Through his uncle Alexander Cochrane he was listed on the books of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> ships from the age of five, a common though unlawful practice known as false muster. His father got him an army commission, but he preferred the navy, which he joined on 23 July 1793 as a midshipman, just as his family lost its wealth and lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A court martial reprimanded him for flippancy toward a superior, the first sign of a pattern of conflict with superiors, colleagues and employers, which led to a long enmity with Admiral John Jervis, Earl of St Vincent. In 1803 he took command of HMS Arab, and in December 1804 of the frigate HMS Pallas, whose cruise near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a> took three Spanish merchant ships and a privateer. His successes led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> to call him le Loup des Mers, &quot;the Sea Wolf&quot;.</p><p>He entered Parliament for Honiton in 1806 on a platform of reform, and in 1807 for Westminster, allied with the Radicals William Cobbett, Sir Francis Burdett and Henry Hunt. His criticism of corruption in the navy and of Admiral Gambier's conduct at the Basque Roads made him powerful enemies.</p><p>In February 1814 a false report of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s death raised share prices, and Cochrane, who had sold his entire holding of Omnium stock, was named as one of six conspirators. After a controversial conviction for fraud he was dismissed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> in 1814. In 1818 he agreed to serve the cause of Spanish American independence and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> on 28 November, where he helped organise the Chilean navy. He went on to lead the navy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in its war of independence against Portugal and, from August 1825, served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, where he met with limited success.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He succeeded to his father's earldom on 1 July 1831 and was restored to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> list as Rear-Admiral of the Blue on 2 May 1832, rising to Admiral of the Red in 1857. He refused any command until his knighthood was restored, which took fifteen years. He died on 31 October 1860 and is buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>; the Brazilian minister laid flowers in the name of the navy he had created. His career inspired the nautical fiction of Frederick Marryat, C. S. Forester's Horatio Hornblower and Patrick O'Brian's Jack Aubrey.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Cochrane, 10th Earl of Dundonald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Chilean navy, commanded from 1818 by the British admiral <b>Thomas Cochrane</b>, then carried San Martín's Liberating Expedition to Peru in 1820, and the last royalist stronghold, the island of Chiloé, fell in 1826.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Imperial forces under Francisco de Lima e Silva and a fleet under <b>Lord Cochrane</b> took Recife in September 1824, and resistance ended by November.</blockquote><a href="/state/confederation-of-the-equator">Confederation of the Equator</a></li><li><blockquote>José de San Martín landed his Liberating Expedition, transported from Chile by <b>Lord Cochrane</b>, on the Peruvian coast in 1820.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-peru">Protectorate of Peru</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hamilton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Kensington, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.78,-4.04&amp;year=1830" aria-label="Hamilton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4462 262 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thomas Cochrane, 10th Earl of Dundonald"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4912 532h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4912 532h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.78,-4.04&amp;year=1830">What was here in 1830? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
